name sghatecheck-annotations
# The README, "/annotations": "This folder contains annotations of selected test cases that were obtained using the
# techniques described in the paper." And "Human annotation of test cases": "A sample of test cases were selected for
# human annotation. For each selected test case, up to three bilingual annotators annotated the test cases as hateful
# if it has harmful language directed at a protected group. Other annotation choices are non-hateful and nonsensical,
# if the test case is incomprehensible to the annotator."
# The columns' names are the files' own. Each annotator column is an annotator of that file's language, and its field
# what that annotator says of the test case; nothing is written between the two. An annotator stands within the file:
# [the set, the file, the column]. The rest is said of the test case by the set.
match annotations/*_annotations.csv

tier row by \n
header
quoted
tier field by ,

thing row c_testcase
key row c_id
key row t_id
# Some files carry the row numbers of the tables they were written out from ("Unnamed: 0", "Unnamed: 0.1", index):
# how they were written down, not what the set says
omit "Unnamed: 0" "Unnamed: 0.1" index
content c_testcase c_group p_label p_value p_target t_case_local t_function t_gold t_direction annotation_common annotation_selected annotator_*
omit c_order
# As in testcases.recipe: the README's figure, "men (placeholder) + I hate {placeholder} (template) = I hate men (test
# case)", and "Placeholders represent the target of each test case". The labels the set gives the case are values of
# its own vocabularies.
value - p_label t_function t_gold t_direction
relation "placeholder" p_value
relation "template" t_case_local
relation "the target of each test case" p_target
# Obligations (Laplace-Engine#32):
#   c_group (only in ta_annotations.csv, 1 to 3): the README says nothing of it, and nothing tells what it groups.
#   annotation_common, annotation_selected (hateful, non-hateful, nonsensical, no_match, undecided): what the
#   annotations came to, but the README words neither, nor how they differ; as bare values they would fold into the
#   gold label t_gold ([case, hateful] once), losing that the annotators said it.
attest row c_group p_label p_value p_target t_case_local t_function t_gold t_direction annotation_common annotation_selected
voices row annotator_* within-file
